y:Ue,horizontal:We,alkalize:Ge,orchil:Ke,burnt:qe,coordain:Je,neatness:Ye,dentolingual:Xe,"gutta-percha":`A concrete juice produced by various trees found in the Malayan archipelago, especially by the Isonandra, or Dichopsis, Gutta. It becomes soft, and unpressible at the tamperature of boiling water, and, on cooling, retains its new shape. It dissolves in oils and ethers, but not in water. In many of its properties it resembles caoutchouc, and it is extensively used for many economical purposes. The Mimusops globosa of Guiana also yields this material.`,prenticeship:Ze,moa:Qe,electrify:$e,"free-spoken":'Accustomed to speak without reserve. Bacon. -- Free"-spo`ken-ness, n.',contentless:et,butyryl:tt,paludamentum:nt,confederacy:rt,exactor:it,beltein:at,consilience:ot,"feeble-minded":`Weak in intellectual power; wanting firmness or constancy; irresolute; vacilating; imbecile. "comfort the feeble-minded." 1 Thess. v. 14. -- Fee"ble-mind"ed*ness, n.`,catena:st,donzel:ct,prothesis:lt,maternally:ut,sassoline:dt,abib:ft,millreis:pt,presbytership:mt,myrmotherine:ht,ostitis:gt,irrationality:_t,monk:vt,superfamily:yt,vitreous:bt,crypt:xt,pterygium:St,benediction:Ct,tapering:wt,concessionary:Tt,perigean:Et,coopery:Dt,flete:Ot,rusticity:kt,soldieress:At,mohammedanism:jt,transfuge:Mt,"muck-rake":`To seek for, expose, or charge, esp. habitually, corruption, real or alleged, on the part of public men and corporations. On April 14, 1906, President Roosevelt delivered a speech on "The Man with the Muck Rake," in which he deprecated sweeping and unjust charges of corruption against public men and corporations. The phrase was taken up by the press, and the verb to muck"rake\`, in the above sense, and the noun muck"rak\`er, to designate one so engaged, were speedily coined and obtained wide currency. The original allusion was to a character in Bunyan's "Pilgrim's Progress" so intent on raking up muck that he could not see a celestial crown held above him.`,torpescence:Nt,placentation:P